      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1782145516775,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Output Embedding Vector",
      "fontSize": 16,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Output Embedding Vector",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "_HgsCvHQjMqcMBE9JWh-2",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 169.47073391435845,
      "y": 2092.847973857142,
      "width": 583.1993408203125,
      "height": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "hachure",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b0j",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 700597124,
      "version": 160,
      "versionNonce": 888575364,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1782098200450,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Q. What is an embedding?\n\nA. Embedding is a K-Dimensional Representation of any entity. For eg,\n    a. story with id=543210 could be represented as [0.23, -0.81, ..., 0.12] \n    b. user with id=90 could be represented as [0.2, 0.44, ..., 0.35]",
      "fontSize": 16,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Q. What is an embedding?\n\nA. Embedding is a K-Dimensional Representation of any entity. For eg,\n    a. story with id=543210 could be represented as [0.23, -0.81, ..., 0.12] \n    b. user with id=90 could be represented as [0.2, 0.44, ..., 0.35]",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "pEpBDrlHTD04ELIhTpqTR",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 187.89960244661916,
      "y": 2857.4338853279605,
      "width": 1151.5673828125,
      "height": 380,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "hachure",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b14",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1858969276,
      "version": 1206,
      "versionNonce": 1829830276,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1782101070702,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Q. Why need a model for this?\n    a. A story is much more complex than these dimensions. Humans can't easily figure out all the key dimensions and unlikely interactions.\n    b. So we train an ML model that can take in a story_id and output a numeric K-dimensional embedding representation\n    c. The model is trained on what users actually clicked, liked, or skipped - so the geometry reflects behavior, not just content\n    d. All stories/users output by the same ML model, will be in the same K-dimensional space and adhere to the meaning represented by the K-axes.\n    e. Outcome: Similar Stories (eg. similar by topics, language, country etc.) will clump together in this K-dimn space.\n   \nQ. What are some real world examples?\n    a. TTSN in Recsys: \n        a. Build the model with user and item interaction data (details in later sessions)\n        b. Pre-compute Story embeddings and store them in an VectorDB like Pinecone. \n        c. Compute User embedding during request time\n        d. Lookup ANN (approximate nearest neighbors) in the story embeddings stored in VectorDB with user embedding.\n        e. This retrieves relevant stories in a User's interest space.\n    b. BERT embeddings:\n        a. Encode a word or text to an embedding\n        b. This is a pretrained model that can be invoked directly\n        c. Input: Sentence -> tokenized -> fed into model. Output: a vector representing it's meaning\n        d. Fine-tuning adapts it to our engagement data - starting from a pretrained checkpoint",
      "fontSize": 16,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Q. Why need a model for this?\n    a. A story is much more complex than these dimensions. Humans can't easily figure out all the key dimensions and unlikely interactions.\n    b. So we train an ML model that can take in a story_id and output a numeric K-dimensional embedding representation\n    c. The model is trained on what users actually clicked, liked, or skipped - so the geometry reflects behavior, not just content\n    d. All stories/users output by the same ML model, will be in the same K-dimensional space and adhere to the meaning represented by the K-axes.\n    e. Outcome: Similar Stories (eg. similar by topics, language, country etc.) will clump together in this K-dimn space.\n   \nQ. What are some real world examples?\n    a. TTSN in Recsys: \n        a. Build the model with user and item interaction data (details in later sessions)\n        b. Pre-compute Story embeddings and store them in an VectorDB like Pinecone. \n        c. Compute User embedding during request time\n        d. Lookup ANN (approximate nearest neighbors) in the story embeddings stored in VectorDB with user embedding.\n        e. This retrieves relevant stories in a User's interest space.\n    b. BERT embeddings:\n        a. Encode a word or text to an embedding\n        b. This is a pretrained model that can be invoked directly\n        c. Input: Sentence -> tokenized -> fed into model. Output: a vector representing it's meaning\n        d. Fine-tuning adapts it to our engagement data - starting from a pretrained checkpoint",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "u3VSmaXG2oaeeB7YMwCTb",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 167.60180651118492,
      "y": 2330.2961065290306,
      "width": 1257.6632080078125,
      "height": 140,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffc9c9",
      "fillStyle": "hachure",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b15",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1559876996,
      "version": 2280,
      "versionNonce": 329401860,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1782145511490,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "\n\nQ. Why do this? \n    a. A story_id (or user_id) has no meaning, it's a random number\n    b. So we create a numeric representation that means something\n    c. In the simplest form, think of it as concrete parameters in each axis. Eg. each story has X-axis as number of words in story, and Y-axis as number of likes\n    d. Notice how stories may start to clump together",
      "fontSize": 16,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "\n\nQ. Why do this? \n    a. A story_id (or user_id) has no meaning, it's a random number\n    b. So we create a numeric representation that means something\n    c. In the simplest form, think of it as concrete parameters in each axis. Eg. each story has X-axis as number of words in story, and Y-axis as number of likes\n    d. Notice how stories may start to clump together",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
