    },
    {
      "id": "6G0OmZ8phskGhQFqn0Pka",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 205.4743869942737,
      "y": 6133.951254940978,
      "width": 1254.3834228515625,
      "height": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "hachure",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b5i",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 793086468,
      "version": 1273,
      "versionNonce": 913047612,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1782325323349,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "2. Even the scorer function from earlier can be packaged as a model. However, that's a better fit as a separate heuristic based layer. \n3. Production systems rely on MTML models to predict ranking scores. Please look for examples such as DCN (Deep and cross Neural Nets) for how these work. \n4. The last piece before returning results is to do a Diversity and Integrity pass. We want to avoid showing the same type of video repeatedly and avoid\n    borderline or already seen content.\n5. Notice the funnel shape from Retrieval to Ranker to Final Passes. This is exactly what a prod system does to get to a fine-tuned response.",
      "fontSize": 16,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "2. Even the scorer function from earlier can be packaged as a model. However, that's a better fit as a separate heuristic based layer. \n3. Production systems rely on MTML models to predict ranking scores. Please look for examples such as DCN (Deep and cross Neural Nets) for how these work. \n4. The last piece before returning results is to do a Diversity and Integrity pass. We want to avoid showing the same type of video repeatedly and avoid\n    borderline or already seen content.\n5. Notice the funnel shape from Retrieval to Ranker to Final Passes. This is exactly what a prod system does to get to a fine-tuned response.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "pHYiCYzf5OY_9UJ44aNJM",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 242.9563239115622,
      "y": 7794.964366441576,
      "width": 1526.574951171875,
      "height": 200,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "hachure",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b5j",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 500997252,
      "version": 3730,
      "versionNonce": 1320258052,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1782325540082,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "\n\nWhere did the model come from?\n\n1. A dedicated training service manages model creation and ongoing retraining\n2. MLE interact with this service (through notebooks or scripts) to trigger model training\n3. Training  service reads training data (features and labels) from storage. The training data is kept it in special formats (eg. parquet) that's easy to process during training.\n4. Once a model is trained, a model is published for the specific hardware used during serving. It's stored back to blob storage.\n5. The model is finally validated and served on the inference service\n6. Ranking systems constantly update model version every few hours based on newly available training data. (eg. new posts, user behavior change etc.). This is called recurring training and serving.",
      "fontSize": 16,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "\n\nWhere did the model come from?\n\n1. A dedicated training service manages model creation and ongoing retraining\n2. MLE interact with this service (through notebooks or scripts) to trigger model training\n3. Training  service reads training data (features and labels) from storage. The training data is kept it in special formats (eg. parquet) that's easy to process during training.\n4. Once a model is trained, a model is published for the specific hardware used during serving. It's stored back to blob storage.\n5. The model is finally validated and served on the inference service\n6. Ranking systems constantly update model version every few hours based on newly available training data. (eg. new posts, user behavior change etc.). This is called recurring training and serving.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "sTmXzgzD1k5tWLXpuPW17",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 237.21166835205167,
      "y": 7542.9668551889,
      "width": 1254.5430908203125,
      "height": 240,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "hachure",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b5k",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 613886212,
      "version": 62,
      "versionNonce": 2127812228,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1782325535519,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Does the current setup scale well?\n\n1. Rather than hold the model on the ranking service, we can move it to it's own dedicated \"Inference Service\"\n2. This lets the components scale independently and maintains the Single Responsbility principle.\n3. The inference service is responsible for model serving and health, often leaniing on GPU for fast processing. \n4. Each query needs the input parameters to the model. These are called Features.\n5. Features are planned specifically for each model and go through a complex compute path (we'll cover this in the next section)\n6. Features are stored in caches for quick fetching during an online query and backed by durable databases.\n7. The Ranker gathers the features for it's query (eg. a user's language and their last_7d_usage pattern, a post's number of likes, average_view_duration etc.)\n8. It makes the query to the model hosted on the inference service\n9. The service exposes API endpoints for the model and returns the result - prediction score of a story's relevance. \n10. This is used to order the news stories so the top stories can be returned to the user",
      "fontSize": 16,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Does the current setup scale well?\n\n1. Rather than hold the model on the ranking service, we can move it to it's own dedicated \"Inference Service\"\n2. This lets the components scale independently and maintains the Single Responsbility principle.\n3. The inference service is responsible for model serving and health, often leaniing on GPU for fast processing. \n4. Each query needs the input parameters to the model. These are called Features.\n5. Features are planned specifically for each model and go through a complex compute path (we'll cover this in the next section)\n6. Features are stored in caches for quick fetching during an online query and backed by durable databases.\n7. The Ranker gathers the features for it's query (eg. a user's language and their last_7d_usage pattern, a post's number of likes, average_view_duration etc.)\n8. It makes the query to the model hosted on the inference service\n9. The service exposes API endpoints for the model and returns the result - prediction score of a story's relevance. \n10. This is used to order the news stories so the top stories can be returned to the user",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
