    {
      "id": "rD3BGRpbl5ati9y4QVvpR",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 149.01445858772337,
      "y": 9826.777276313991,
      "width": 513.9957885742188,
      "height": 35,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "hachure",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b9N",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1576268676,
      "version": 733,
      "versionNonce": 747676676,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1782327099587,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Step 7: Processing all the information",
      "fontSize": 28,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Step 7: Processing all the information",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "rOI-_r96mz7PiFl_Mw53p",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 177.49920584965736,
      "y": 9902.536563639389,
      "width": 1743.6309814453125,
      "height": 300,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "hachure",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b9O",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 498262788,
      "version": 3836,
      "versionNonce": 455740420,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1782328997956,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "We have been hand-wavy with how data is processed for these generators.. Lets dig into that next.\n\n1. All user requests, interactions, engagement and impression data is sent back from the website / app as API calls to the Server\n2. This goes through to a dedicated Frontend Service which is responsible for the business logic.\n3. The service writes these data points out to a message queue as multiple downstream components are waiting for them. This is better than writing to a DB and letting services poll/query independently\n4. The message queue is the spine of the whole system. It carries info across the system and it needs to be a reliable component. Kafka is battle-tested and scalable option\n5. Several consumers can listen on kafka for messages relevant to them\n    a. Stream processing service (eg. Flink) reads live engagement/impression events (eg. num_views) and aggregates in real-time\n    b. Content Understanding service looks for new content/comment creation signals to process them (eg. produce embeddings and store to IndexingEngine or vectorDB)\n    c. Data Consumer service reads raw data and writes out to a blob storage for training data, audit logs and observability metrics. \n6. The stream processing path is interesting and often trades of accuracy for speed. It writes some data directly to the db (eg. story_clicks_last_5_min) and passes some info to the Joiner\n7. Joiner is a critical component to create Training data with labels. It combines the data during time of prediction with the actual event that happens.\n8. While processing a request, the Ranker writes the features at the time and predicted score for each story and user to the Joiner. The joiner keeps this in memory for minutes to hours.\n9. When a user interacts with the story (dwells on the story or likes or comments), the Joiner listens to the event and matches the previous entry it created. This is how it builds training data with labels and stores them.\n10. We additionally need Batch Processing (eg. spark) to periodically compute exact data points and to create features based on larger time windows. This compensates for the stream processing accuracy gaps.",
      "fontSize": 16,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "We have been hand-wavy with how data is processed for these generators.. Lets dig into that next.\n\n1. All user requests, interactions, engagement and impression data is sent back from the website / app as API calls to the Server\n2. This goes through to a dedicated Frontend Service which is responsible for the business logic.\n3. The service writes these data points out to a message queue as multiple downstream components are waiting for them. This is better than writing to a DB and letting services poll/query independently\n4. The message queue is the spine of the whole system. It carries info across the system and it needs to be a reliable component. Kafka is battle-tested and scalable option\n5. Several consumers can listen on kafka for messages relevant to them\n    a. Stream processing service (eg. Flink) reads live engagement/impression events (eg. num_views) and aggregates in real-time\n    b. Content Understanding service looks for new content/comment creation signals to process them (eg. produce embeddings and store to IndexingEngine or vectorDB)\n    c. Data Consumer service reads raw data and writes out to a blob storage for training data, audit logs and observability metrics. \n6. The stream processing path is interesting and often trades of accuracy for speed. It writes some data directly to the db (eg. story_clicks_last_5_min) and passes some info to the Joiner\n7. Joiner is a critical component to create Training data with labels. It combines the data during time of prediction with the actual event that happens.\n8. While processing a request, the Ranker writes the features at the time and predicted score for each story and user to the Joiner. The joiner keeps this in memory for minutes to hours.\n9. When a user interacts with the story (dwells on the story or likes or comments), the Joiner listens to the event and matches the previous entry it created. This is how it builds training data with labels and stores them.\n10. We additionally need Batch Processing (eg. spark) to periodically compute exact data points and to create features based on larger time windows. This compensates for the stream processing accuracy gaps.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "5jVpc8cqYP9TsMkmNQ_x1",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 72.96554992700715,
      "y": 6374.060381449022,
      "width": 533.539794921875,
      "height": 35,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "hachure",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b9P",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1100114108,
      "version": 753,
      "versionNonce": 2037129220,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1782325510949,
      "link": null,
