    {
      "id": "LK3hCI4Ul-qGYGfiPPwP3",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 398.367108321504,
      "y": 12115.429262321017,
      "width": 1983.7584228515625,
      "height": 250,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#b2f2bb",
      "fillStyle": "hachure",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "dotted",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bBr",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1902423612,
      "version": 1535,
      "versionNonce": 1377347900,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1782408939681,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "How do we know that our changes are improving the system?\n\n1. All these changes sound great on paper, but may not always work\n2. We need a solid experimentation framework to measure the positive and negative impact of any change\n3. Experimentation frameworks run A/B tests where a control arm is production code (eg. heuristic based ranking or old model) and test arm is the new experimental code (eg. a new model)\n4. We send a small percentage of real traffic through both arms of this experiment (control vs test) to check how they perform.\n5. They plug into many services in the call flow and alter the path for specific users (eg. userA is shown results from model_A, while userB gets results from model_B)\n6. The outcome is measured by metrics tracking topline (eg. daily active users, session duration), guardrails (eg. integrity, latency, error metrics) and experiment specific metrics (eg. user behavior shifts).\n7. Users are split across Experimentation universes which further split them into segments to avoid pollution from other related experiments.\n8. Experimentation helps teams identify good changes and help justify their launch to production",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "How do we know that our changes are improving the system?\n\n1. All these changes sound great on paper, but may not always work\n2. We need a solid experimentation framework to measure the positive and negative impact of any change\n3. Experimentation frameworks run A/B tests where a control arm is production code (eg. heuristic based ranking or old model) and test arm is the new experimental code (eg. a new model)\n4. We send a small percentage of real traffic through both arms of this experiment (control vs test) to check how they perform.\n5. They plug into many services in the call flow and alter the path for specific users (eg. userA is shown results from model_A, while userB gets results from model_B)\n6. The outcome is measured by metrics tracking topline (eg. daily active users, session duration), guardrails (eg. integrity, latency, error metrics) and experiment specific metrics (eg. user behavior shifts).\n7. Users are split across Experimentation universes which further split them into segments to avoid pollution from other related experiments.\n8. Experimentation helps teams identify good changes and help justify their launch to production",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
