    {
      "id": "mw_gCJfqDxgp16zKaWRKx",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 207.80513784812558,
      "y": 14485.486163894619,
      "width": 1867.6588134765625,
      "height": 900,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#b2f2bb",
      "fillStyle": "hachure",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "dotted",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bLI",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 744310660,
      "version": 6881,
      "versionNonce": 1008471612,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1782411556387,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "How do we scale this for Production traffic?\n\n1. When we hear the word scale, we need to think of sharding and horizontal scaling. Only in rare instances does vertical scaling (increasing resources like CPU/RAM in the service) make sense.\n2. Highlighted in the diagram are key software services that are scaled\n    a. Load Balancer: Added in the entry point to the service and in multiple places across the system when services talk to each other\n    b. Cache: Cache data to avoid costly recomputation wherever possible. Often slightly stale results are well-tolerated.\n    c. Metadata Service: Wrap a service responsible for gathering metadata or configs. Let it maintain sync with the database and handle simple logic.\n    d. Logger Service: Separation of concerns to free up the Frontend service. This lets frontend focus on business logic and offload metrics to another service.\n3. Training Service:\n    a. This deserves a dedicated chapter. For now, we can think of it as a black box that reads data in and writes a model out.\n    b. Control Plane:\n        (i) MLE setup configurations (model code, training data table, data time-range, training hardware, publish target etc)\n        (ii) The control plane manages where each training job runs and how to efficiently schedule jobs across teams on available hardware\n    c. Data Preprocessing (DPP):\n        (i) Each model runs several data fetch jobs to retrieve data (more DPP workers for higher scale of data fetch)\n        (ii) This should be tuned to avoid starving or overwhelming the core training workers\n    d. Workers:\n        (i) These are the actual machines where the training is done\n        (ii) Depending on the model size, this could be sharded in various ways to produce a final model\n            A. Data Parallel — model fits on one host. Run multiple copies, send each a different chunk of data, average gradients across them. Faster training, same model.\n            B. Model Parallel — model itself doesn't fit on one host. Two flavors:\n                (1) Tensor Parallel — split individual layers across hosts; each host computes part of the same operation.\n                (2) Pipeline Parallel — split the model by layer ranges across hosts; data flows through hosts in sequence, like an assembly line.\n            C. Hybrid Parallelism — combine multiple options at once; what large-scale model training actually uses in practice.\n4. Inference Service:\n    a. Also deserves a dedicated chapter later. For now, think of it as a black box that runs many models. Pass in features, get back predictions.\n    b. Control Plane:\n        (i) Manages how and where models are deployed using user configurations (hardware to run on, number of replicas, datacenters to run on etc.)\n        (ii) Monitors model health, scaling and maintains the SLA guarantee on latency/error\n        (iii) Frameworks to handle model launch, model snapshot transitions and efficiency tracking\n    c. Runtime:\n        (i) Actual deployment of the model. Typically on a container and running a specific inference runtime that model is published for.\n        (ii) Receives input parameters and does preprocessing or batching (if necessary)\n        (iii) Executes the graph using the inputs and produces the final result to return\n5. The choices we made for other services are battle tested components that scale (kafka, flink, cassandra, redis, etc,)\n6. All of these services will be deployed in sharded and horizontally scaled forms that process a subset of the requests/data.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "How do we scale this for Production traffic?\n\n1. When we hear the word scale, we need to think of sharding and horizontal scaling. Only in rare instances does vertical scaling (increasing resources like CPU/RAM in the service) make sense.\n2. Highlighted in the diagram are key software services that are scaled\n    a. Load Balancer: Added in the entry point to the service and in multiple places across the system when services talk to each other\n    b. Cache: Cache data to avoid costly recomputation wherever possible. Often slightly stale results are well-tolerated.\n    c. Metadata Service: Wrap a service responsible for gathering metadata or configs. Let it maintain sync with the database and handle simple logic.\n    d. Logger Service: Separation of concerns to free up the Frontend service. This lets frontend focus on business logic and offload metrics to another service.\n3. Training Service:\n    a. This deserves a dedicated chapter. For now, we can think of it as a black box that reads data in and writes a model out.\n    b. Control Plane:\n        (i) MLE setup configurations (model code, training data table, data time-range, training hardware, publish target etc)\n        (ii) The control plane manages where each training job runs and how to efficiently schedule jobs across teams on available hardware\n    c. Data Preprocessing (DPP):\n        (i) Each model runs several data fetch jobs to retrieve data (more DPP workers for higher scale of data fetch)\n        (ii) This should be tuned to avoid starving or overwhelming the core training workers\n    d. Workers:\n        (i) These are the actual machines where the training is done\n        (ii) Depending on the model size, this could be sharded in various ways to produce a final model\n            A. Data Parallel — model fits on one host. Run multiple copies, send each a different chunk of data, average gradients across them. Faster training, same model.\n            B. Model Parallel — model itself doesn't fit on one host. Two flavors:\n                (1) Tensor Parallel — split individual layers across hosts; each host computes part of the same operation.\n                (2) Pipeline Parallel — split the model by layer ranges across hosts; data flows through hosts in sequence, like an assembly line.\n            C. Hybrid Parallelism — combine multiple options at once; what large-scale model training actually uses in practice.\n4. Inference Service:\n    a. Also deserves a dedicated chapter later. For now, think of it as a black box that runs many models. Pass in features, get back predictions.\n    b. Control Plane:\n        (i) Manages how and where models are deployed using user configurations (hardware to run on, number of replicas, datacenters to run on etc.)\n        (ii) Monitors model health, scaling and maintains the SLA guarantee on latency/error\n        (iii) Frameworks to handle model launch, model snapshot transitions and efficiency tracking\n    c. Runtime:\n        (i) Actual deployment of the model. Typically on a container and running a specific inference runtime that model is published for.\n        (ii) Receives input parameters and does preprocessing or batching (if necessary)\n        (iii) Executes the graph using the inputs and produces the final result to return\n5. The choices we made for other services are battle tested components that scale (kafka, flink, cassandra, redis, etc,)\n6. All of these services will be deployed in sharded and horizontally scaled forms that process a subset of the requests/data.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
