      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1782413040366,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "How do we make this reliable for Production traffic?\n\n1. Each of our services needs to be replicated, preferably across datacenters. The system components chosen (kafka, redis, cassandra etc) already do this to maintain durability for the data.\n2. They use concepts like checkpointing and periodically save state to resume in case of hardware/network failures.\n3. Each Infra service must commit to an SLA metric that it can achieve for a given amount of traffic. This is usually an Error Rate and Latency SLA commitment. \n4. Monitoring and Alerting need to be set to ensure this is met. If not, investigations must be done to restore performance to SLA levels.\n5. Fallback Service:\n    a. When results fail for unavoidable reasons, we need a backup. This is the fallback service\n    b. It uses past cached results for a user or simple logic to find results to return \n    c. This ensures the user almost never has zero results\n    d. In addition, caching is introduced at various levels including on the user device to prevent a scenario where no results are found\n6. Offline or Batch Ranking\n    a. This is used to enhance results from both main ranking and Fallback\n    b. Offline ranker runs asynchronously to rank stories for users. It follows a similar path as online Ranking Service, but during off-peak hours or an opportunistic capacity (like AWS spot instances)\n    b. It could be another candidate generator and as a high quality data source during fallbacks\n7. Validation\n    a. When Training Service publishes a model or new snapshot version, it needs to be validated before deployment to production\n    b. The model goes through several tests to check\n        (i) model files are not corrupt and in expected formats for the hardware deployment\n        (ii) model can serve within performance limits (latency, error rate, serving qps) \n        (iii) model outputs on golden data is within quality limits expected\n        (iv) optional shadow traffic to check output and scaling\n        (v) canary to small percentage of traffic and then to one datacenter\n        (vi) full rollout and health monitoring        \n    c. This gauntlet ensures the model is highly reliable in production serving. Any failures result in rollback to working versions.\n8. Reliability is a huge area and we've only scratched the surface here. Each service needs its own checks and balances to ensure reliability of the whole system.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "How do we make this reliable for Production traffic?\n\n1. Each of our services needs to be replicated, preferably across datacenters. The system components chosen (kafka, redis, cassandra etc) already do this to maintain durability for the data.\n2. They use concepts like checkpointing and periodically save state to resume in case of hardware/network failures.\n3. Each Infra service must commit to an SLA metric that it can achieve for a given amount of traffic. This is usually an Error Rate and Latency SLA commitment. \n4. Monitoring and Alerting need to be set to ensure this is met. If not, investigations must be done to restore performance to SLA levels.\n5. Fallback Service:\n    a. When results fail for unavoidable reasons, we need a backup. This is the fallback service\n    b. It uses past cached results for a user or simple logic to find results to return \n    c. This ensures the user almost never has zero results\n    d. In addition, caching is introduced at various levels including on the user device to prevent a scenario where no results are found\n6. Offline or Batch Ranking\n    a. This is used to enhance results from both main ranking and Fallback\n    b. Offline ranker runs asynchronously to rank stories for users. It follows a similar path as online Ranking Service, but during off-peak hours or an opportunistic capacity (like AWS spot instances)\n    b. It could be another candidate generator and as a high quality data source during fallbacks\n7. Validation\n    a. When Training Service publishes a model or new snapshot version, it needs to be validated before deployment to production\n    b. The model goes through several tests to check\n        (i) model files are not corrupt and in expected formats for the hardware deployment\n        (ii) model can serve within performance limits (latency, error rate, serving qps) \n        (iii) model outputs on golden data is within quality limits expected\n        (iv) optional shadow traffic to check output and scaling\n        (v) canary to small percentage of traffic and then to one datacenter\n        (vi) full rollout and health monitoring        \n    c. This gauntlet ensures the model is highly reliable in production serving. Any failures result in rollback to working versions.\n8. Reliability is a huge area and we've only scratched the surface here. Each service needs its own checks and balances to ensure reliability of the whole system.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
